// FU1.schema — the OLDER side of the fixed form's TEXT-UNDER-AN-ARM pair
// (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §3.4, §15). It exists because of a hole nothing else in
// the corpus reached: FE1/FE2 slides a union's ordinals, FX1/FX2 edits fields,
// and the paired bench corpus carries a three-arm union — but NOT ONE of them
// puts a `string(N)` INSIDE A UNION ARM, and a plan entry that has to carry
// both "which arm am I guarded by" and "which flavour of text am I" is exactly
// where a single overloaded lane goes wrong. It went wrong (reference-fix 12,
// found on the Dart leg): a `string(N)` in a union's SECOND arm read as ''
// through a compiled plan while the identity read landed the text, with no
// counter and no refusal to say so.
//
// So the fixture is the shape the bug needs: a union whose SECOND arm carries a
// string(8) with a SCALAR BEFORE AND AFTER it, so a mislaid length or a
// mislaid guard moves a neighbour too and the equality check sees it. The
// scalar in the FIRST arm keeps the arms different sizes, which is what makes
// the second arm's offsets its own.
//
//
// IT CARRIES TWO MORE FIELDS THAN THE BUG STRICTLY NEEDS, AND THEY LEAD THE
// BODY SO THEIR BYTES ARE AT A KNOWN OFFSET: a `bool` and an optional. §3.4
// spells both as ONE BYTE THAT IS TRUE WHEN IT IS NOT ZERO — not when it is
// one — so a writer that spells `true` 2, or presence 7, is a writer this
// reader still understands. That is a ruling and not an accident, so this
// fixture is where it is held: the test forges those two bytes and reads them
// back down BOTH paths, which is the same pair of paths the text above needs.
//
// AND TWO WIDENING RUNGS RIDE THE SAME FIXTURE, AFTER `tail` so the two bytes
// above keep their offsets: `mark` and `heat` (see below).
//
// FU2 is the same declaration with one field appended, so its layout hash
// differs and a read of FU1's bytes goes through a COMPILED plan rather than
// the identity one. That is the whole point: the two reads have to agree field
// for field, and the text is a field.
package tblfu1

type Plain
{
    n int32 = 7
}

type Labelled
{
    lead  int32 = 5
    label string(8)
    trail int32 = 9
}

union Pick
{
    plain    Plain
    labelled Labelled
}

table FuRoot
{
    flag bool = false
    note ?int32
    pick Pick
    tail int32 = 3
    // THE SIGNED WIDENING RUNG (SPEC §4), which nothing else in this corpus
    // reaches: FX1/FX2 widens a `uint16` into a `uint32` and every other pair
    // moves fields around. A signed rung is its own code path — the source is
    // read SIGN-EXTENDED at the writer's width and re-imaged at mine — and a
    // rung that only ever carried positive numbers would pass while reading
    // the sign wrong. FU2 declares the same field an `int32`.
    mark int16 = -1
    // AND THE FLOAT RUNG (SPEC §4), f32 into f64, for the same reason: it is
    // the one rung that is not a re-imaged integer, and the pattern it has to
    // get right is a SIGNALLING NaN — which the reference's `(double) f`
    // QUIETS, because every hardware convert does. FU2 declares it a `float64`.
    heat float32 = 0
}
